# Pediatric red-cell interval and discriminator evidence scan

## TL;DR

The strongest openly retrievable interval carrier I found for the analytes your module lacks is the CALIPER DxH 900 study: it provides pediatric reference intervals for **RBC count, hematocrit, MCH, and MCHC** on a named analyzer, with age and sex partitions, in healthy children and adolescents. It is open and numerically retrievable. citeturn25search0turn27search0

On the “definition” question, the most important public-domain exception is **CDC’s 1998 MMWR**, which published pediatric anemia cutoffs for **both hemoglobin and hematocrit**. By contrast, WHO defines anemia by **hemoglobin concentration** on its current public page, and current NHLBI/AAP materials I found do not publish pediatric hematocrit diagnostic cutoffs as the definition of anemia. citeturn47search1turn45search1turn46search1turn45search0

For derived indices, the key negative finding is that the **traditional adult reticulocyte production index is not validated for children**. Bracho and Osorio found **no agreement at any pediatric age** between the traditional adult-normalized RPI and an age-adjusted pediatric RPI, with clinically meaningful disagreement in anemia classification. citeturn39search4turn40view0

For hereditary spherocytosis, **high MCHC can be discriminative in pediatric cohorts**, but the same run also surfaced strong published reasons to fail closed on automated MCHC when there are sample/interference signals: **cold agglutinins, lipemia, hemolysis, cryoglobulins, and very high WBC counts** can all produce spurious CBC relationships. citeturn28search4turn28search7turn28search10turn29search4turn29search0turn30search4turn30search3

## Context and source-selection

I prioritized sources in this order: openly retrievable pediatric interval studies; public-domain federal carriers; then paywalled abstracts only where the abstract itself exposed the exact threshold or performance number. That produced one clear “best” open interval carrier for the missing red-cell analytes: **CALIPER Hematology Reference Standards I** on the **Beckman Coulter DxH 900**, which reports age- and sex-specific pediatric intervals for RBC count, hematocrit, MCH, and MCHC. citeturn25search0turn27search0

I also found a second high-value, independently retrievable open carrier using **NHANES** data and piecewise regression. In the search-accessible excerpt, it exposes pediatric **hematocrit, MCH, MCHC, hemoglobin, and MCV** intervals and is especially valuable if you want an openly retrievable U.S.-based carrier for hemoglobin or MCV that could supersede paywalled rows already in your system. I did **not** use it as the primary Table 1 carrier below because the CALIPER DxH 900 tables exposed the missing analytes more completely in this run. citeturn20search0turn21search0

