| AAP | no | none | current AAP childhood iron-deficiency screening material accessible in this run uses CBC/ferritin, with hemoglobin as the minimum fallback | AAP News 2026; `https://publications.aap.org/aapnews/news/35298/AAP-report-on-iron-deficiency-includes-expanded` | paywalled | “If a complete blood count and serum ferritin cannot be obtained, at a minimum hemoglobin should be assessed.” |
| ASH | partial | none published in accessible snippet | age- and sex-adjusted ranges; no explicit society cutoff table exposed in accessible snippet | Blood review 2022; `https://ashpublications.org/blood/article-abstract/140/6/571/484178` | paywalled | “In practice, anemia is defined by hemoglobin (Hb), hematocrit, or red blood cell count levels lower than the normal age- and sex-adjusted ranges.” |

## Synthesis and limits

For your missing **input-threshold substrate**, the cleanest open interval insertion point from this run is the **CALIPER DxH 900** carrier for `rbc`, `hematocrit`, `mch`, and `mchc`. It is analyzer-specific, pediatric, age/sex partitioned, and numerically retrievable. The main implementation caveat is not evidentiary but structural: some source bands straddle your scope boundaries, so any downstream use must preserve the source partitions and explicitly truncate outside **6 months to <18 years** rather than silently re-bin or extend them. citeturn25search0turn27search0

For **corrected reticulocyte percentage / RPI**, the strongest answer from this search is negative: adult-normalized RPI is not safe to import unchanged into pediatrics. The open Bracho study provides age-adjusted normal hematocrit denominators and maturation-factor breakpoints, but it simultaneously concludes that the traditional RPI is **not an adequate pediatric tool** because age-adjusted and traditional results do not agree. That means an auto-computed pediatric RPI would still need a cautious verifier stance even if you add hematocrit thresholds. citeturn39search4turn40view0

For **MCHC-driven hereditary-spherocytosis logic**, the evidence supports candidate rows, not an unguarded rule. Pediatric studies do show useful thresholds, but the literature also makes clear that spurious high MCHC can arise from **cold agglutinins, lipemia, hemolysis, cryoglobulins, and hyperleukocytosis**, which means any production logic around MCHC should fail closed when CBC internal consistency breaks or specimen-quality/interference flags are present. citeturn28search4turn28search7turn28search10turn29search4turn29search0turn30search4turn30search3

For the **Hgb↔Hct “rule of three”**, I found openly retrievable laboratory-training/QC usage as an **internal consistency check**, but I did **not** find WHO, CDC, NHLBI, or current AAP guidance endorsing it as a clinical interpretation rule for pediatric anemia. On the evidence located here, it belongs in the “QC/check-for-artifact” bucket, not the “derive patient-facing hematocrit or interpret anemia clinically” bucket. citeturn43search1turn43search3turn45search1turn46search1turn45search0